      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "οὐ γὰρ ἂν ὁ τὰ πάντα γινώσκων θεὸς (ὡς ἐκ πολλῶν ἔστιν δεῖξαι) ἐπείραζεν, ἵνα γνῷ αὐτός, ὁ τὰ πάντα προγινώσκων.",
          "english_quote": "For God, who knows everything, would not test in order to learn for himself, since he knows everything beforehand, as can be shown from many passages.",
          "issue": "The parenthetical qualification follows God's knowing everything in the Greek. Moving it to the sentence's end changes the clause order and makes it read as support specifically for the final foreknowledge statement.",
          "suggested_revision": "Restore the parenthesis's placement: “For God, who knows everything (as can be shown from many passages), would not test in order to learn for himself, since he knows everything beforehand.”",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "οὔτε ἀπὸ ·γραφῶν οὔτε ὑφ’ ἑτέρου τινὸς σκανδαλισθῆναι δυνάμεθα οὔτε πολλοὺς θεοὺς ἀποδέξασθαι ἀπατώμεθα",
          "english_quote": "we can neither be made to stumble by scriptures or anyone else, nor be deceived into accepting many gods",
          "issue": "The English extends “can” over both coordinated predicates. In the Greek, δυνάμεθα governs being made to stumble, whereas ἀπατώμεθα independently states that they are not deceived into accepting many gods. The draft strengthens that second assertion into an inability to be deceived.",
          "suggested_revision": "Distinguish the predicates: “Thus, Simon, we cannot be made to stumble either by scriptures or by anyone else; neither are we deceived into accepting many gods, nor do we agree with any statement spoken against God.”",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "16.14",
