      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "λέγω δὲ ὡς ὁ ἐπὶ τῆς βάτου λαλήσας καὶ τῷ Ἰακὼβ παλαίσας, ἀλλὰ καὶ τὸν γεννώμενον Ἐμμανουὴλ καὶ τὸν λεγόμενον θεὸν ἰσχυρόν.",
          "english_quote": "I mean the one who spoke at the bush and the one who wrestled with Jacob, as well as the one born as Emmanuel and the one called Mighty God.",
          "issue": "The first two participles share a single article, ὁ, naturally presenting one subject who spoke and wrestled. The draft repeats “the one,” presenting them as independently enumerated figures and obscuring the contrast with the subsequently repeated τὸν. Although the English does not necessarily assert distinct identities, the material shared-subject reading is not clearly represented or discussed in the notes.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use “I mean the one who spoke at the bush and wrestled with Jacob, as well as the one born as Emmanuel and the one called Mighty God.” Explain that the first two participles share one article and that this motivates the shared-subject rendering. If retaining a distributive interpretation instead, document its grammatical basis and preserve the shared-subject alternative without asserting necessarily distinct identities.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
